/-! # Étale linearization of a regular immersion

Source: literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-smooth-purity/sk-prop-an/
lem-pur-regular-immersion-linearization.md (blueprint: lem-pur-regular-immersion-linearization).

The theorem states the smoothness of the regular closed subscheme and the étale local
coordinate normal form. Its proof follows the regular-smooth criterion, the conormal
basis, the differential rank theorem, and the étale differential criterion listed in
the DAG node.
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits AlgebraicGeometry

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A regular closed immersion into a smooth finite-type scheme is étale-locally a
coordinate linear immersion. Neighborhoods are pointed étale neighborhoods, with unchanged
residue field at a chosen lift. The coordinate-subspace map is the actual `homOfVector`
with its first `c` coordinates zero and its last `m` coordinates the standard ones.
Source: blueprint `sec-smooth-purity/sk-prop-an`,
`lem-pur-regular-immersion-linearization`, clauses 1 and 2
(blueprint: lem-pur-regular-immersion-linearization).

Proof (the five steps of the DAG node):
1. The fixed field is an algebraic closure of the finite field, hence perfect. The
   finite-type regular-smooth criterion (`sk-lem-regular-smooth`) makes `Z` smooth.
   Fix an arbitrary point `z`, not necessarily closed, and put `x = i(z)`.
2. Take an affine chart `Spec A` about `x`; its closed preimage is `Spec (A/J)`
   (`def-weil-closed-immersion`). Finite type makes `A` Noetherian
   (`lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian`), hence `J` finite. The stalkwise
   regular sequence of length `c` gives a conormal basis
   (`lem-weil-pbc-regular-sequence-conormal-free`). Represent its elements by `f_i/s_i`.
   Multiply by the units `s_i` and shrink to make `f_i` generate `J`: the finite module
   `J/(f_i)` has zero stalk, so invert a product of annihilators of its generators.
3. Smooth differentials on `Z` have rank `m = dim_z Z`
   (`lem-weil-pbc-smooth-differentials-free-rank-fibre-dimension`). They are generated by
   differentials of functions (`def-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials` and
   `def-sc-relative-differential-sheaf`); choose `m` whose residue classes are a basis.
   Differential localization (`lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-localization`) permits
   representing these germs on a common affine neighborhood. Lift the chosen functions
   `g_j` through the quotient to the ambient chart, shrinking if necessary. The short
   exact conormal fibre sequence (`lem-pur-conormal-sequence-smooth-immersion`) shows
   that `df_i,dg_j` form a basis: project to the quotient for independence, and lift and
   subtract for spanning.
4. Form the actual map `(f_i,g_j)` to affine `(c+m)`-space. The chosen-coordinate
   criterion (`lem-pur-etale-at-point-from-differentials`) gives an open neighborhood
   `U` on which this map is étale. The equality `J = (f_i)` survives restriction.
5. Set `V = Z ×_X U`. Equality of the ideals on affine charts identifies this closed
   subscheme with the scheme-theoretic inverse image of the displayed coordinate
   subspace. Étale base change (`lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change`) makes the
   restricted map étale. The neighborhood inclusions are open, hence étale
   (`lem-weil-pbc-open-immersion-etale`), contain the chosen points, and induce
   residue-field isomorphisms. Their kernel ideal sheaf is generated by the `f_i`.
-/
theorem regularImmersionLinearization
    (P : WeilConjectures.PurityFiniteSetting.{u})
    {X Z : Scheme.{u}}
    (hX : X ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k))
    (i : Z ⟶ X)
    [IsSeparated hX] [LocallyOfFiniteType hX] [QuasiCompact hX]
    [IsSeparated (i ≫ hX)] [LocallyOfFiniteType (i ≫ hX)] [QuasiCompact (i ≫ hX)]
    [IsClosedImmersion i]
    (c : ℕ)
    (hreg : IsRegularImmersionOfPureCodimension hX i c)
    [Smooth hX]
    (hZreg : ∀ z : Z, ∃ W : Z.affineOpens, z ∈ W.1 ∧ IsRegularRing Γ(Z, W.1)) :
    Smooth (i ≫ hX) ∧
      ∀ (z : Z), ∃ (m : ℕ),
        topologicalKrullDimAt z = (m : WithBot ℕ∞) ∧
        ∃ (U V : Scheme.{u}) (uU : U ⟶ X) (vV : V ⟶ Z)
          (vU : V ⟶ U) (u : U ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin (c + m)))
            (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k)))
          (w : V ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin m))
            (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k))),
          Etale uU ∧ Etale vV ∧ Etale u ∧ Etale w ∧
          u ≫ (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin (c + m)))
            (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k)) ↘ Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k)) =
              uU ≫ hX ∧
          (∃ zu : U, uU zu = i z ∧ IsIso (uU.residueFieldMap zu)) ∧
          (∃ zv : V, vV zv = z ∧ IsIso (vV.residueFieldMap zv)) ∧
          IsPullback vU vV uU i ∧
          IsPullback vU w u
            (AffineSpace.homOfVector
              (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin m)) (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k)) ↘
                Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k))
              (fun r : ULift.{u} (Fin (c + m)) ↦
                Fin.addCases (fun _ : Fin c ↦ 0)
                  (fun s : Fin m ↦
                    AffineSpace.coord (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k)) (ULift.up s))
                  r.down)) ∧
          vU.ker = Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop
            (Ideal.span (Set.range (fun r : Fin c ↦
              u.appTop (AffineSpace.coord (Spec (CommRingCat.of P.field.k))
                (ULift.up (Fin.castAdd m r)))))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
